# Explore the Finite Library of Babel

A book with $L$ positions over an alphabet of $A$ symbols is one point in a library of $A^L$ possibilities. Begin by changing those two parameters and watching a finite number become astronomical.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The [Hamming distance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamming_distance) between two books counts their differing positions. Distinct books are always at least distance one apart, so balls of radius one half isolate single books. The resulting topology is discrete, totally disconnected, and zero-dimensional.

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the topological argument</summary>
Every singleton is an open ball of radius one half. Its complement is a union of singleton open sets, so it is closed as well. These clopen singletons form a basis. Any subset containing two points is separated by one singleton and its complement; hence every connected subset has at most one point. A genuine library with at least two symbols and one position is therefore disconnected.
</details>

## Neighborhood geometry

A radius-$r$ Hamming ball contains $\sum_{j=0}^r  inom Lj(A-1)^j$ books. Explore the shells, then inspect the exact counting procedure.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

For background on why these neighborhoods matter in communication, see [error-correcting codes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Error_correction_code).

## Why short descriptions cannot cover everything

Any decoder with $N$ codes has at most $N$ outputs. Therefore at least $A^L-N$ books remain unnamed. In the binary case, exact $(L-c)$-bit codes cover at most a $2^{-c}$ fraction.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the counting proof and its scope</summary>
Restrict the decoder's codomain to its image. This gives a surjection from the code set onto the described books, so the image cannot be larger than the code set. Subtract its size from $A^L$. This is decoder-relative: exact [Kolmogorov complexity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolmogorov_complexity) requires a fixed machine and is generally uncomputable. Programs of all lengths below a threshold must also be counted differently from programs of one exact length.
</details>

## Continuous controls versus symbolic outputs

A continuous image of a connected space is connected, but the only connected subsets of this library are singletons. Thus every continuous decoder from a connected parameter space is constant; a system producing multiple exact books must introduce a discrete decision.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Explore the broader mathematical landscape</summary>
Finite Hamming libraries are discrete. Infinite streams with the product topology behave differently: binary streams form [Cantor space](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantor_space), which is compact and totally disconnected but has no isolated points. This contrast motivates the infinite-library and probability directions described in the research paper.
</details>

